[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
**Short answer:** No. For a store-locator with ~50 points you can (and should) keep things simple and use a **GeoJSON source**. A vector-tile server would be over-engineering for this size of dataset.

---

## Why GeoJSON is the right choice here

| Metric | Typical range | What you get |
|--------|---------------|--------------|
| **Feature count** | 50 ≈ << 5 000 | Well inside the “sweet-spot” for GeoJSON |
| **File size** | < 10 KB (even with properties) | Far below the 2 MB threshold where parsing starts to feel noticeable |
| **Browser work** | One download + JSON parse | Instant load, no extra tile requests, no extra server to maintain |
| **Interactivity** | Full client-side access to every feature | Easy click/hover pop-ups, live updates, filtering, clustering, etc. |
| **Future growth** | Even if you double- or triple-size you stay comfortably under the “lag zone” | No need to re-architect until you hit tens of thousands of points |

The **GeoJSON performance thresholds** in the skill state:

| Range | Size / features | Behaviour |
|------|----------------|-----------|
| Sweet spot | < 2 MB / < 5 000 features | Instant load, smooth interaction |
| Lag zone | 5–20 MB / up to ~50 000 features | 1–3 s parse delay, mobile may struggle |
| Crash zone | > 50 MB / > 100 000 features | Browser freeze / crash – switch to vector tiles |

Your 50-store dataset is orders of magnitude smaller than the sweet-spot ceiling, so you’ll get **instant loading** and **full client-side control** without any tile infrastructure.

---

## How to wire it up

### 1. Prepare the GeoJSON

```json
{
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "features": [
    {
      "type": "Feature",
      "properties": {
        "name": "Main Street Store",
        "address": "123 Main St, Anytown, USA",
        "phone": "555-123-4567"
      },
      "geometry": {
        "type": "Point",
        "coordinates": [-122.4194, 37.7749]   // lon, lat
      }
    }
    // …repeat for the other 49 locations…
  ]
}
```

*You can keep this file on your web server (`/data/stores.geojson`) or embed it directly in the script as a JavaScript object.*

### 2. Add a **vector basemap** (optional but typical)

Most store-locator apps want a background map (roads, terrain, labels). Use any public vector-tile style URL, e.g.:

```javascript
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_KEY', // vector basemap
  center: [-98.5795, 39.8283], // continental US
  zoom: 4
});
```

> **Tip:** If you need a raster satellite background, swap the style URL for a raster source (or add a raster layer on top of the vector basemap). The choice of basemap does **not** affect the decision about your store data source.

### 3. Add the GeoJSON source & layer

```javascript
map.on('load', () => {
  // 1️⃣ Add the GeoJSON source
  map.addSource('stores', {
    type: 'geojson',
    data: '/data/stores.geojson'   // or `data: myGeojsonObject`
  });

  // 2️⃣ Add a layer to draw the points
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'store-points',
    type: 'circle',
    source: 'stores',
    paint: {
      'circle-radius': 8,
      'circle-color': '#ff6600',
      'circle-stroke-width': 2,
      'circle-stroke-color': '#fff'
    }
  });

  // 3️⃣ Optional: add a label layer for store names
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'store-labels',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: 'stores',
    layout: {
      'text-field': ['get', 'name'],
      'text-offset': [0, 1.2],
      'text-anchor': 'top',
      'text-size': 12
    },
    paint: {
      'text-color': '#000'
    }
  });

  // 4️⃣ Interaction – show a popup on click
  map.on('click', 'store-points', (e) => {
    const props = e.features[0].properties;
    new maplibregl.Popup()
      .setLngLat(e.lngLat)
      .setHTML(`
        <strong>${props.name}</strong><br/>
        ${props.address}<br/>
        ${props.phone}
      `)
      .addTo(map);
  });

  // Change cursor on hover
  map.on('mouseenter', 'store-points', () => map.getCanvas().style.cursor = 'pointer');
  map.on('mouseleave', 'store-points', () => map.getCanvas().style.cursor = '');
});
```

**Result:** The map loads the basemap tiles *once* (vector or raster) and then fetches a single tiny GeoJSON file (≈ 10 KB). All 50 store points are instantly interactive.

---

## When you *might* consider vector tiles (future-proofing)

| Situation | Why you’d switch |
|-----------|------------------|
| **> 100 000 points** or **> 50 MB** GeoJSON | Parsing becomes a bottleneck; tiles load only what’s visible |
| **Zoom-dependent styling** (e.g., cluster at low zoom, detailed icons at high zoom) | Vector tiles let you define different layers per zoom level without re-sending the whole dataset |
| **Offline / air-gapped deployment** where you need a single archive | A PMTiles/MLT file can be bundled and served locally; still no server needed, but you’d generate tiles once |
| **Strict bandwidth limits** (e.g., mobile-only users on a metered plan) | Vector tiles are far smaller than a full GeoJSON payload at higher zooms |

If any of those become true later, you can **add a vector tile source** for the stores without touching the basemap:

```javascript
map.addSource('stores-tiles', {
  type: 'vector',
  url: 'pmtiles://https://example.com/tiles/stores.pmtiles' // or TileJSON URL
});
```

But for the current 50-store use-case, that extra step is unnecessary.

---

## TL;DR checklist for your store locator

1. **Use a vector (or raster) basemap style URL** – no need to host it yourself unless you have licensing or branding constraints.
2. **Add your stores as a `geojson` source** – either a static file or an inline object.
3. **Create a point (or symbol) layer** to render the stores and attach click/hover pop-ups.
4. **Deploy** – only two HTTP requests: the basemap style (which pulls its own tiles) and the tiny GeoJSON file.

That’s it. No vector-tile server, no extra build pipeline, and you’ll have a fast, fully interactive store locator across the United States. Happy mapping!

Below is a complete, step-by-step recipe for turning a single scanned JPG (or any georeferenced raster image) into a **MapLibre `image` source**, adding it as a raster layer, and giving the user a simple opacity toggle.  
The pattern follows the “image source” entry in the **source-type table** of the *maplibre-tile-sources* skill and is the recommended way when you have just one raster file and the exact corner coordinates.

---

## 1. Why an `image` source is the right choice

| Situation | Recommended source type | Reason |
|-----------|------------------------|--------|
| One static raster (JPG/PNG/TIFF) whose four corners you know | **`image`** | No tiling needed, MapLibre will warp the image to the supplied corner coordinates. |
| Large raster that must stay sharp at many zoom levels | `raster` tiles (or PMTiles) | Image source would be stretched; tiling keeps resolution. |
| You need to edit the raster on the fly (e.g., draw on it) | `image` + canvas overlay | Still start with an `image` source. |

Because you have a single JPG and the corner lat/longs, the `image` source is the simplest, most performant solution. It loads the whole file once, then the browser draws it as a raster layer that can be styled (opacity, brightness, contrast, etc.) and toggled on/off.

---

## 2. Prepare the image URL

* Host the JPG where the browser can fetch it (same origin or CORS-enabled).  
  *If you are developing locally, put it in your `public/` folder (e.g. `public/historic-1920.jpg`).*  

```text
https://mydomain.com/assets/historic-1920.jpg
```

If the image is on a different domain, make sure that domain sends the header:

```
Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
```

or the specific origin of your page, otherwise the image will be blocked by the browser.

---

## 3. Define the corner coordinates

MapLibre expects the coordinates in **counter-clockwise order** starting with the **top-left** corner:

```js
// [lon, lat] for each corner
const imageCorners = [
  [westLng, northLat], // top-left
  [eastLng, northLat], // top-right
  [eastLng, southLat], // bottom-right
  [westLng, southLat]  // bottom-left
];
```

> **Tip:** If you only have the four corner points in a different order, just reorder them to match the pattern above. The image will be warped to fit exactly.

---

## 4. Add the `image` source and a raster layer

```js
// Assuming you already have a MapLibre map instance called `map`
map.on('load', () => {
  // 1️⃣ Add the image source
  map.addSource('historic-map', {
    type: 'image',
    url: 'https://mydomain.com/assets/historic-1920.jpg',
    coordinates: imageCorners   // <-- the array from step 3
  });

  // 2️⃣ Add a raster layer that uses the source
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'historic-map-layer',
    type: 'raster',
    source: 'historic-map',
    paint: {
      // Start semi-transparent so the basemap is still visible
      'raster-opacity': 0.7
    }
  }, 
  // Insert *before* the first symbol layer so labels stay on top.
  // If you don’t know the first symbol layer, you can compute it:
  // const firstSymbolId = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol').id;
  // then pass that id as the second argument.
  firstSymbolId);
});
```

### Why we use a **raster** layer on top of an **image** source

* The `image` source only supplies the raster data and its georeferencing.  
* The `raster` layer is the only layer type that can render an `image` source, and it gives you access to raster-specific paint properties (`raster-opacity`, `raster-brightness-min`, `raster-contrast`, etc.).

---

## 5. Opacity toggle UI

You can expose a simple HTML range input (slider) or a checkbox that changes the `raster-opacity` paint property.

### Example: Slider (0 – 100 %)

```html
<label for="opacity-slider">Historic map opacity</label>
<input id="opacity-slider" type="range" min="0" max="100" value="70">
```

```js
const slider = document.getElementById('opacity-slider');

slider.addEventListener('input', (e) => {
  const opacity = Number(e.target.value) / 100; // convert 0-100 → 0-1
  map.setPaintProperty('historic-map-layer', 'raster-opacity', opacity);
});
```

### Example: Checkbox (on/off)

```html
<label>
  <input type="checkbox" id="historic-toggle" checked>
  Show 1920s map
</label>
```

```js
const checkbox = document.getElementById('historic-toggle');

checkbox.addEventListener('change', (e) => {
  const visibility = e.target.checked ? 'visible' : 'none';
  map.setLayoutProperty('historic-map-layer', 'visibility', visibility);
});
```

Both approaches work; you can combine them (checkbox to hide/show, slider to adjust opacity while visible).

---

## 6. Layer ordering – keep labels readable

If you add the raster layer **after** the basemap’s label layers, the historic map will cover the labels, which is usually undesirable. The pattern shown in the code above inserts the layer **before** the first symbol layer:

```js
// Find the first symbol layer in the current style
const firstSymbolId = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol').id;

// Pass that id as the second argument to addLayer()
map.addLayer(rasterLayerDefinition, firstSymbolId);
```

If you are using a custom style that already groups label layers under a known id (e.g., `road-labels`), you can insert before that id directly.

---

## 7. Optional: Adding a fade-in effect

If you want the historic map to fade in when the user toggles it on, you can animate the opacity:

```js
function fadeIn(layerId, duration = 500) {
  let start = null;
  const step = (timestamp) => {
    if (!start) start = timestamp;
    const progress = Math.min((timestamp - start) / duration, 1);
    map.setPaintProperty(layerId, 'raster-opacity', progress * 0.7);
    if (progress < 1) requestAnimationFrame(step);
  };
  requestAnimationFrame(step);
}

// Call fadeIn('historic-map-layer') when the checkbox becomes checked.
```

---

## 8. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Historic Map Overlay</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.3.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.3.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
    .control { position: absolute; top: 10px; left: 10px; background: white; padding: 8px; border-radius: 4px; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<div class="control">
  <label for="opacity">Opacity</label>
  <input id="opacity" type="range" min="0" max="100" value="70">
</div>

<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any vector basemap
    center: [ -122.447303, 37.753574 ], // example center
    zoom: 12
  });

  // ---- 1️⃣ Corner coordinates (replace with your own) ----
  const imageCorners = [
    [-122.511, 37.812], // top-left (west, north)
    [-122.381, 37.812], // top-right (east, north)
    [-122.381, 37.704], // bottom-right (east, south)
    [-122.511, 37.704]  // bottom-left (west, south)
  ];

  map.on('load', () => {
    // ---- 2️⃣ Add the image source ----
    map.addSource('historic-map', {
      type: 'image',
      url: 'https://mydomain.com/assets/historic-1920.jpg',
      coordinates: imageCorners
    });

    // ---- 3️⃣ Insert before first symbol layer ----
    const firstSymbolId = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol').id;

    // ---- 4️⃣ Add the raster layer ----
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'historic-map-layer',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'historic-map',
      paint: { 'raster-opacity': 0.7 }
    }, firstSymbolId);
  });

  // ---- 5️⃣ Opacity slider UI ----
  const opacitySlider = document.getElementById('opacity');
  opacitySlider.addEventListener('input', (e) => {
    const opacity = Number(e.target.value) / 100;
    map.setPaintProperty('historic-map-layer', 'raster-opacity', opacity);
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace the `imageCorners` array and the `url` with your own data.* The example uses the public MapLibre demo style as a basemap, but any vector or raster basemap works the same way.

---

## 9. When you might need a different approach

| Situation | Better alternative |
|-----------|--------------------|
| You need the historic map to stay crisp when users zoom far beyond the native resolution of the JPG. | Slice the image into a **raster tile set** (XYZ or PMTiles) and add it as a `raster` source. |
| You want to let users draw on the historic map or query pixel values. | Load the image as a **canvas source** (custom source) or use a WebGL shader overlay. |
| You have dozens of historic maps and want to switch between them quickly. | Store each as a separate `image` source and toggle the layer’s `visibility`, or bundle them into a single **PMTiles** archive with separate layer IDs. |

For a single, static scanned map, the `image` + `raster` layer combo is the cleanest, simplest, and most performant solution.

---

### TL;DR Checklist

1. **Host the JPG** (CORS enabled).  
2. **Create an `image` source** with the four corner `[lon, lat]` pairs in counter-clockwise order.  
3. **Add a `raster` layer** that uses that source; set an initial `'raster-opacity'`.  
4. **Insert the layer before the first symbol layer** so labels stay on top.  
5. **Expose UI** (slider or checkbox) that calls `map.setPaintProperty` / `map.setLayoutProperty` to change `'raster-opacity'` or `'visibility'`.  

That’s all you need to overlay a historic scanned map on a MapLibre GL JS map and let users control its opacity. Happy mapping!

**Use **`vector` **tiles for the basemap**  

A single vector-tile source can be styled in any number of ways on the client, so you can have a light-theme style and a dark-theme style that both point at the *same* tile URL. Switching the style (or just swapping a few paint properties) changes the map’s appearance instantly, without needing a second set of raster tiles.

### Why vector tiles are the right choice

| Requirement | Raster tiles | Vector tiles |
|-------------|--------------|--------------|
| Two visual themes (light / dark) | Need two separate raster tile sets (one per theme) → double storage & bandwidth | One tile set; the style (colors, label opacity, etc.) is applied in the browser |
| Runtime theme switch | Must reload a different tile URL → flicker, extra requests | Call `map.setStyle()` or toggle paint properties → instant, no extra tile fetches |
| Ability to tweak colors, label visibility, line widths, etc. | Fixed – you’d have to regenerate raster tiles for every change | Fully controllable via the style JSON (or programmatically) |
| Feature interactivity (click/hover, queries) | Not possible – raster tiles have no feature data | Features are queryable (`queryRenderedFeatures`, `querySourceFeatures`) |
| Bandwidth efficiency | Larger image tiles (PNG/JPEG) for every zoom level | Compact MVT/MLT tiles (often 5-10 × smaller than equivalent raster) |

So the answer is **vector tiles**.

---

## How to implement a light/dark switch with a single vector source

1. **Pick a vector-tile provider** (e.g. OpenStreetMap-derived tiles from MapTiler, OpenFreeMap, or your own self-hosted tiles).  
   The TileJSON URL will look like `https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf`.

2. **Create two style JSONs** (or one style that you modify at runtime):
   * `style-light.json` – light background, muted colors.
   * `style-dark.json` – dark background, high-contrast colors.

   Both styles contain the *same* source definition, e.g.:

   ```json
   "sources": {
     "basemap": {
       "type": "vector",
       "url": "https://example.com/tiles.json"
     }
   }
   ```

   The only differences are in the layer `paint`/`layout` sections (background color, road colors, label text color, etc.).

3. **Load the map with one of the styles**:

   ```js
   const map = new maplibregl.Map({
     container: 'map',
     style: 'style-light.json',   // start with light theme
     center: [0, 0],
     zoom: 2
   });
   ```

4. **Add a UI control to toggle the theme**:

   ```js
   const toggle = document.getElementById('theme-toggle');
   toggle.addEventListener('click', () => {
     const newStyle = map.getStyle().metadata?.theme === 'dark'
       ? 'style-light.json'
       : 'style-dark.json';

     // Preserve the current view (center, zoom, bearing, pitch)
     const cam = map.getCamera(); // map.getCenter(), getZoom(), etc.

     map.setStyle(newStyle);

     // When the new style finishes loading, restore the view
     map.once('styledata', () => {
       map.jumpTo(cam);
     });
   });
   ```

   *Tip:* Store a custom `metadata.theme` field in each style JSON so you can read the current theme without extra state.

5. **Alternative: single style + dynamic paint changes**  
   If you prefer not to load two full style documents, you can keep one style and just toggle the relevant paint properties:

   ```js
   function setTheme(isDark) {
     const bg = isDark ? '#111' : '#fff';
     const road = isDark ? '#555' : '#aaa';
     const label = isDark ? '#ddd' : '#222';

     map.setPaintProperty('background', 'background-color', bg);
     map.setPaintProperty('road-primary', 'line-color', road);
     map.setPaintProperty('road-label', 'text-color', label);
     // repeat for any other layers you want to recolor
   }

   // UI hook
   document.getElementById('theme-toggle')
           .addEventListener('click', () => setTheme(!currentIsDark));
   ```

   This approach avoids a full `setStyle` call and keeps the same source and layer order intact.

### What you **don’t** need

- Two separate raster tile services (one light, one dark).  
- Two separate vector-tile services (the same vector data can be reused).  
- Any server-side re-rendering when the user flips the switch.

---

## Quick checklist

- [ ] **Vector source** (`type: "vector"`), TileJSON URL points to a single tile set.  
- [ ] **Two style JSONs** (or one style with runtime paint changes).  
- [ ] **Metadata flag** (optional) to identify which theme a style represents.  
- [ ] **UI control** that calls `map.setStyle()` (or `setPaintProperty`) and restores the camera.  
- [ ] **Test on low-bandwidth / mobile** – you’ll see only one set of tile requests regardless of theme.

---

### TL;DR

> **Answer:** Use a **vector-tile basemap**. One vector source can be styled in both light and dark modes on the client, letting you switch themes at runtime without hosting two separate tile collections.

**Short answer:**  
Yes – but not with plain Leaflet alone. Leaflet’s core can only draw raster tiles, so to show a **MapLibre-style vector-tile basemap** you need to embed a tiny MapLibre GL JS map inside the Leaflet map. The officially-supported way to do that is the **`maplibre-gl-leaflet`** plugin.

Below is a step-by-step guide, plus a few “gotchas” you’ll run into the first time you try it.

---

## 1. Why you need a plugin

| Library | What it can render out-of-the-box |
|---------|-----------------------------------|
| **Leaflet** | Raster tiles (XYZ, WMTS, WMS) only. No vector-tile decoding or style evaluation. |
| **MapLibre GL JS** | Vector tiles (or raster tiles) **and** full style JSON. Renders everything client-side. |
| **maplibre-gl-leaflet** | A thin wrapper that creates a MapLibre GL canvas as a Leaflet layer, letting you keep the rest of your Leaflet UI (markers, pop-ups, controls, etc.) while the basemap is rendered by MapLibre. |

So the workflow is:

```
Leaflet map (L.map)  ←→  maplibre-gl-leaflet layer (L.maplibreGL)  ←→  MapLibre style / vector tiles
```

---

## 2. Install the three pieces you need

You can pull them from a CDN (quick prototype) or install via npm/yarn for a production build.

### CDN (quick start)

```html
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css" />
<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>

<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" />
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl-leaflet@0.0.13/dist/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>
```

> **Version tip:** Keep the three libraries roughly in sync. `maplibre-gl-leaflet` is built against a specific major version of MapLibre GL JS; if you upgrade one, check the plugin’s `peerDependencies` and bump the other if needed.

### npm / Yarn (production)

```bash
npm i leaflet maplibre-gl maplibre-gl-leaflet
# or
yarn add leaflet maplibre-gl maplibre-gl-leaflet
```

Then import in your bundler (ESM, CommonJS, Vite, etc.):

```js
import L from 'leaflet';
import 'leaflet/dist/leaflet.css';

import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

import 'maplibre-gl-leaflet';   // registers L.maplibreGL
```

---

## 3. Create the Leaflet map **first**

```js
// Choose any CRS you like – the default EPSG:3857 works for most web maps.
const leafletMap = L.map('map', {
  center: [40.7128, -74.0060], // New York City
  zoom: 12,
  // optional: make the MapLibre canvas sit *below* Leaflet overlays
  // (default is above, which is fine for most cases)
  // preferCanvas: true,
});
```

Add any Leaflet UI you already have (controls, markers, etc.) **before** you add the MapLibre layer, or you can add them later – the order only matters for visual stacking (see § 5).

---

## 4. Add the MapLibre-GL-as-Leaflet layer

```js
// 1️⃣  Choose a style URL. You can use any public style that points to vector tiles.
//     Example: MapTiler’s free “basic” style, or your own hosted style.json.
const styleUrl = 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/basic/style.json?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY';

// 2️⃣  Create the layer. The plugin accepts the same options you would give to
//     maplibregl.Map, plus a few Leaflet-specific ones.
const glLayer = L.maplibreGL({
  // MapLibre GL options:
  style: styleUrl,
  // optional: if you need to pass an access token or custom headers:
  // transformRequest: (url, resourceType) => ({
  //   url,
  //   headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${myToken}` },
  // }),

  // Leaflet-specific options:
  //   `attribution` – will be merged into the Leaflet attribution control.
  attribution: '&copy; <a href="https://www.maptiler.com/">MapTiler</a> contributors',
  //   `zIndex` – controls stacking relative to other Leaflet layers.
  //   `pane` – you can create a custom pane if you need fine-grained control.
}).addTo(leafletMap);
```

That’s it – the basemap now renders as a **single Leaflet layer** that behaves like any other overlay (you can toggle it on/off, change its opacity, etc.).

### 4.1. Controlling the layer order

Leaflet draws layers in the order they are added, **unless you use panes**. If you have Leaflet markers or vector layers that must appear *above* the basemap (the usual case), add those **after** the `glLayer`, or give the `glLayer` a lower `zIndex`/pane:

```js
// Create a pane for the MapLibre basemap (z-index 200)
leafletMap.createPane('basemapPane');
leafletMap.getPane('basemapPane').style.zIndex = 200;

// Add the GL layer to that pane
L.maplibreGL({ style: styleUrl, pane: 'basemapPane' }).addTo(leafletMap);

// Later, add a marker – it will be in the default overlay pane (z-index 400)
L.marker([40.7128, -74.0060]).addTo(leafletMap);
```

---

## 5. Mixing MapLibre layers with Leaflet overlays

Because the MapLibre canvas is just another DOM element, you can freely combine:

| Leaflet element | Typical use | Stacking tip |
|-----------------|-------------|--------------|
| `L.marker`, `L.circle`, `L.polygon` (Leaflet vector) | Points, user-drawn shapes, pop-ups | Add **after** the GL layer (or use a higher-zIndex pane). |
| `L.tileLayer` (raster) | Satellite imagery, WMTS | If you need raster *on top* of the vector basemap, add it **after** the GL layer. |
| `L.control` | UI widgets | Unaffected – they sit in the Leaflet control container. |
| `L.popup` | Click/hover info | Works as usual; you can query MapLibre features (see § 6) and then open a Leaflet popup. |

---

## 6. Querying features from the vector tiles

One of the main reasons you want a vector-tile basemap is **interactivity** (clickable roads, label lookup, etc.). The plugin exposes the underlying `maplibregl.Map` instance via `glLayer.getMap()`:

```js
leafletMap.on('click', async function (e) {
  const maplibreMap = glLayer.getMap();

  // Convert Leaflet latLng → MapLibre point (pixel coordinates)
  const point = maplibreMap.project([e.latlng.lat, e.latlng.lng]);

  // Query rendered features at that point (you can filter by source-layer)
  const features = maplibreMap.queryRenderedFeatures(point, {
    layers: ['road', 'water', 'building'] // optional: limit to specific layers
  });

  if (features.length) {
    const f = features[0];
    L.popup()
      .setLatLng(e.latlng)
      .setContent(`<strong>${f.layer.id}</strong>: ${JSON.stringify(f.properties)}`)
      .openOn(leafletMap);
  }
});
```

**Important:** The query runs **client-side**, so you only get features that are actually rendered at the current zoom (the same rule that applies in a pure MapLibre map). If you need *all* features regardless of zoom, you would have to host a separate GeoJSON or vector-tile endpoint and query it via HTTP.

---

## 7. Handling CORS & Attribution

* **CORS** – The vector-tile server (or style JSON) must allow cross-origin requests from your domain. Most public providers (MapTiler, OpenMapTiles, MapLibre-hosted styles) already send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`. If you self-host, configure your web server (nginx, Apache, CloudFront, etc.) accordingly.

* **Attribution** – The plugin automatically merges the `attribution` option you pass into the Leaflet attribution control. If the style JSON already contains an `attribution` field, you can omit it here; the plugin will read it from the style and add it for you.

---

## 8. Performance considerations

| Factor | What to watch for | Mitigation |
|--------|-------------------|------------|
| **Canvas size** | The MapLibre canvas is the same size as the Leaflet map container. If you have many Leaflet overlays (e.g., thousands of markers), the DOM can become heavy. | Use marker clustering (`leaflet.markercluster`) or render many points as a MapLibre vector layer instead of Leaflet markers. |
| **Tile requests** | Vector tiles are fetched per-source. The plugin creates **one source** (the style URL) – the same as a standalone MapLibre map. | Keep the style lean: limit the number of source-layers you actually need, and use `source-layer` filters in your style to drop unused data. |
| **GPU memory** | MapLibre GL uses WebGL; on low-end mobile devices you may hit texture limits if the style includes many raster layers. | Prefer vector-only basemaps; if you need raster imagery, use a low-resolution raster source or a `raster-dem` for terrain only. |
| **Interaction latency** | Querying rendered features is cheap, but if you fire many queries per mouse move you can saturate the main thread. | Debounce or throttle `mousemove` events; use `querySourceFeatures` for non-rendered data when possible. |

---

## 9. Full minimal example (HTML)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>Leaflet + MapLibre GL basemap</title>
  <style>
    html, body, #map { height: 100%; margin: 0; }
  </style>

  <!-- Leaflet -->
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css">
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>

  <!-- MapLibre GL -->
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css">
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

  <!-- Plugin -->
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl-leaflet@0.0.13/dist/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>
</head>

<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // 1️⃣ Leaflet map
  const map = L.map('map', {
    center: [40.7128, -74.0060],
    zoom: 12
  });

  // 2️⃣ MapLibre GL basemap layer
  const glLayer = L.maplibreGL({
    style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/basic/style.json?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY',
    attribution: '&copy; <a href="https://www.maptiler.com/">MapTiler</a> contributors'
  }).addTo(map);

  // 3️⃣ Example Leaflet marker on top
  L.marker([40.7128, -74.0060])
    .bindPopup('Hello from Leaflet!')
    .addTo(map);

  // 4️⃣ Click-to-query MapLibre features
  map.on('click', async e => {
    const mlMap = glLayer.getMap();
    const point = mlMap.project([e.latlng.lat, e.latlng.lng]);
    const feats = mlMap.queryRenderedFeatures(point, { layers: ['road', 'water'] });
    if (feats.length) {
      L.popup()
        .setLatLng(e.latlng)
        .setContent(`<pre>${JSON.stringify(feats[0].properties, null, 2)}</pre>`)
        .openOn(map);
    }
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

